WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.331
<v Riley>Hey there — today we're deep in the backcountry. A grizzly attack on Queest Mountain, an off-leash dog, and a study putting the injury rate in those encounters at seventy percent. Plus B.C.'s stalled Species at Risk Act and an undercover investigator cracking wildlife trafficking rings. The question: does staying safe out there come down to your own gear, or the systems watching your back? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:43.806 --> 00:01:04.782
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Friday, September 4th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life.

00:01:05.782 --> 00:01:14.942
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're out in the backcountry — the animals that live there, what's tracking them, and what happens when the systems meant to protect them go quiet.

00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:40.074
<v Casey>Quick weather check first. Horsefly Lake is sitting at 11 degrees this morning with light drizzle, high of 16, low of 11. It rains most of the day, a hundred percent chance of precipitation, about six millimetres. Across the region: 100 Mile House around 9 with overcast skies, Williams Lake 12 with light drizzle, Quesnel 12 and overcast, Tatla Lake 11 and overcast. Grey and damp pretty much everywhere.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:42.489
<v Riley>Bring a jacket. Let's get into the news.

00:01:43.889 --> 00:02:02.505
<v Riley>Good stretch of stories today, and a few of them talk to each other in ways worth catching — starting close to home. A Quesnel man has been sentenced in provincial court after swinging a weapon at people in Prince George. He pleaded guilty to robbery and aggravated assault and received 900 days in jail.

00:02:03.178 --> 00:02:06.898
<v Casey>That's a heavy sentence for a fairly quiet story. Worth noting and moving on.

00:02:08.339 --> 00:02:29.171
<v Riley>Sheldon Clare, the Conservative MLA for Prince George-North Cariboo, is criticizing the provincial NDP for policies he says are making B.C. sawmills less competitive. His quote: "People in the Cariboo are tired of NDP excuses while good-paying jobs disappear." He's calling on both Victoria and Ottawa to stop adding costs to the industry.

00:02:30.514 --> 00:02:37.698
<v Casey>Long on outrage, short on which specific policy he means. Standard pre-election messaging from a riding that's lost mill jobs before.

00:02:38.996 --> 00:02:53.764
<v Riley>The Cariboo Chilcotin Orienteering Club put out an invitation for the Labour Day weekend — orienteering is navigation on foot, map and compass, finding checkpoints scattered through unmarked terrain. No trail markers, no phone signal required.

00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:58.055
<v Casey>The literal opposite of a GPS collar. Good counter-programming for today's theme.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:13.498
<v Riley>It's a low-cost way to build the exact skill set that matters when the satellite communicator battery dies fifteen kilometres from the truck. If you want backcountry competence without buying gear first, that club is the entry point.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:26.748
<v Casey>Quesnel's Rona location ran the Lowe's Heroes fundraising campaign, September 1st through October 7th, on behalf of a local charity. Total raised: two thousand two hundred and ninety dollars and fifty cents.

00:03:28.235 --> 00:03:34.771
<v Riley>Modest number, but that's real dollars landing with a real local organization, not a head office press release.

00:03:36.004 --> 00:03:48.708
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Tribune is reporting that the man convicted of killing Langford teenager Kimberly Proctor back in 2010 has been moved to a minimum-security facility. The family is described as shocked by the transfer.

00:03:49.992 --> 00:03:57.960
<v Riley>That's a corrections-system story, not really ours to unpack further — but it explains why it's landing in local papers again sixteen years later.

00:03:59.153 --> 00:04:18.289
<v Casey>And in Kamloops, Prime Minister Carney and the Premier are responding to escalating trade tension with the United States. Carney says talks with Washington resume only once the U.S. drops proposals he says would cripple Canadian industry, and he's been notably blunt about what he called meme-driven attacks coming out of the current administration.

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:29.238
<v Riley>That's the kind of story that feels distant from Horsefly until you remember where Cariboo lumber actually gets sold, and what a tariff wall does to mill payrolls here.

00:04:30.438 --> 00:05:00.878
<v Riley>From there the roundup moves into fire, and specifically what happens after the flames are out. The Tyee has a long piece on business owners trying to rebuild after wildfires. One case: an inn owner whose buildings are now assessed at roughly two point seven million dollars, gone, and she's weighing whether rebuilding is even worth pursuing given how long funding approvals take. The piece frames her as one of dozens of British Columbians who've lost a business to extreme weather over the past decade.

00:05:02.341 --> 00:05:20.925
<v Casey>Global News has the companion piece — residents of Faulder were finally let back onto their properties after the Bald Range fire, walking through what the fire left standing and what it didn't. Two different fires, same arithmetic on the other side: insurance claims, permitting, contractor waitlists, years before a wall goes back up.

00:05:22.173 --> 00:05:45.669
<v Riley>The Logging and Sawmilling Journal is asking a harder question underneath both of those stories — whether forestry contractors, not just firefighters, hold the real key to protecting communities from the next one. Their argument is that decades of aggressive fire suppression changed the fuel load on the landscape and made today's fires bigger and hotter than they'd otherwise be.

00:05:47.148 --> 00:05:57.268
<v Casey>That's the paradox nobody likes saying out loud. Protect the land from fire for eighty years running, and you've quietly built the conditions for a much bigger fire than the one you were preventing.

00:05:58.443 --> 00:06:17.163
<v Riley>APTN News reports the first bowhead whale harvest in Iqaluit since 2022 — a crew of roughly thirty hunters and trappers spent eight days tracking and landing a whale that can weigh up to a hundred tonnes. Community members describe the meat arriving as a genuine event, after a four-year gap.

00:06:18.291 --> 00:06:28.307
<v Casey>Different ocean, different animal, but the same underlying story as everything else today — a community's relationship with a population that isn't guaranteed to be there every season.

00:06:29.513 --> 00:06:42.769
<v Riley>Al Jazeera reports scientists are warning the coming El Niño cycle for 2026 into 2027 could become the strongest ever recorded, which would intensify heat, drought, and flooding on a global scale.

00:06:43.991 --> 00:06:51.375
<v Casey>Which loops straight back to the fire coverage. Hotter baseline, drier fuel, more of exactly what the Logging Journal piece was describing.

00:06:52.705 --> 00:07:03.849
<v Riley>On a more practical note, Wildfire Today reports that prescribed burning duties are now officially eligible for hazardous duty pay under a newly finalized rule covering U.S. wildland firefighters.

00:07:05.207 --> 00:07:13.375
<v Casey>Prescribed burning is the actual antidote to the fuel-load problem, and it's dangerous work in its own right. Good that the pay finally reflects that.

00:07:14.823 --> 00:07:27.639
<v Riley>TechRadar is reporting Thomson Reuters was hit by a cyberattack, with court documents across eleven U.S. states accessed after a hacker compromised the company's cloud environment through its C-Track file system.

00:07:28.817 --> 00:07:35.777
<v Casey>Not backcountry, but the same lesson underneath — a system looks solid right up until somebody tests the weak seam nobody was watching.

00:07:36.959 --> 00:07:48.623
<v Riley>ScienceDaily reports New Mexico's largest reservoir, Elephant Butte, dropped to just one point four percent capacity in July, after years of extreme drought compounded by record early snowmelt.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:53.226
<v Casey>One point four percent isn't a decline. That's basically an empty bathtub.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:08:10.706
<v Riley>And Canadian Architect covers new research on whether cities actually generate more rainfall of their own. The effect on precipitation is real, researchers say, but after correcting for satellite sampling error, it's a smaller effect than earlier studies suggested.

00:08:12.002 --> 00:08:16.770
<v Casey>A story where the finding is "less dramatic than we thought." Rare enough this week that I'll take it.

00:08:17.959 --> 00:08:23.671
<v Riley>That's the roundup. Something a little smaller and closer to home before we head into the deep dive.

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:53.207
<v Casey>This one's a favourite around here. Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is volunteer-run through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists — birding walks, kids' programs, and a Salmon in the Classroom initiative that's been running for years. Their Salmon Trip takes students out for three days at Gavin Lake doing actual field science: brood stock collection, stream surveys, real hands-on work on salmon habitat, not a worksheet with a photo of a fish on it. Applications are open now at scoutisland dot ca.

00:08:53.742 --> 00:09:02.406
<v Riley>That's exactly the kind of science education that sticks with a kid — doing the survey themselves instead of reading someone else's numbers off a page.

00:09:02.932 --> 00:09:12.652
<v Casey>Which is a decent way into what we're covering next, because today's deep dive is about exactly that gap — between reading the data and actually being out where the animals are.

00:09:14.052 --> 00:09:30.084
<v Riley>A man was hiking the Queest Mountain Forest Service Road in the Shuswap yesterday with his dog off-leash, and a grizzly attacked him. He survived. Non-life-threatening injuries, according to conservation officers. On its own, that's a scary local story with a lucky ending.

00:09:30.669 --> 00:09:59.069
<v Casey>The dog is the story underneath it. There's a 2026 study — Backpacker covered it — that put the injury rate for hikers with off-leash dogs in bear encounters at around seventy percent. Far higher than hikers without dogs. And it's not a fringe claim. Parks Canada has an internal document, obtained through access to information, stating flatly that dogs are, quote, "a well recognized important risk factor for serious bear-human contact encounters and attacks." They named Banff specifically.

00:09:59.569 --> 00:10:21.601
<v Riley>Which gets at the real question underneath today's material. When something goes wrong in the backcountry, is the fix a piece of gear one hiker carries and controls — bear spray, a leash, a satellite communicator? Or does it only work if there's a system behind the individual — enforced rules, posted warnings, someone tracking where the bears actually are this week?

00:10:21.914 --> 00:10:42.306
<v Casey>My answer's sitting in that seventy percent. That's not a gear problem. It's a behaviour pattern. An off-leash dog runs at a bear, gets scared, and sprints back toward its owner — and now the bear is coming too, at close range, at a person who had no idea any of this was happening thirty seconds earlier. Bear spray in a hip holster doesn't touch that sequence.

00:10:42.898 --> 00:10:55.210
<v Riley>Bear spray doesn't prevent the encounter, that's true. It changes the outcome once the encounter's already underway. Those are two different jobs, and it's not fair to grade one against a standard it was never built to meet.

00:10:55.864 --> 00:11:18.496
<v Casey>Sure, but the article doesn't even say whether this man had bear spray on him. What we know is he survived, and we know his dog was off-leash on a road that a broader review — The Cool Down covered 326 dog-bear encounters across the U.S. — found raises conflict risk specifically because of that owner-flight dynamic. The dog didn't help him. The dog is the likely reason he needed help at all.

00:11:19.079 --> 00:11:34.191
<v Riley>Fair, we don't have his gear list. But you're describing an upstream failure — the leash decision — as if it cancels out the value of downstream preparedness. They're both real. A hiker who leashes the dog AND carries spray is safer than one who does either alone.

00:11:34.799 --> 00:11:51.087
<v Casey>Nobody's arguing against carrying spray. I'm arguing that "carry the right gear" is the story people in wild-spaces country like to tell about their own competence, and it quietly skips past the actual leverage point, which is a rule that either exists and gets enforced on that road, or doesn't.

00:11:51.487 --> 00:12:21.615
<v Riley>That's close to what happened in Nelson — we touched on it a couple of days ago. WildSafeBC's coordinator there stepped down after the grizzly deaths in her area. That's precisely the role you're describing. Someone whose entire job is watching bear activity, pushing out warnings, working the conflict prevention side that no individual hiker can replicate on their own. When that seat sits empty, nothing collapses loudly. The region just quietly reverts to hikers reading the situation for themselves.

00:12:22.261 --> 00:12:34.141
<v Casey>And nobody replaces that role fast, because there's no ribbon to cut when you rehire a bear-conflict coordinator. It's unglamorous, ongoing labour, and it disappears from budget priority the moment the news cycle moves on.

00:12:34.827 --> 00:12:49.323
<v Riley>It might not even be a budget gap so much as a staffing one — WildSafeBC runs mostly through regional coordinators paired with volunteer networks already. The infrastructure exists. It's a vacancy problem, not necessarily a funding problem.

00:12:49.965 --> 00:13:06.909
<v Casey>Doesn't change the outcome for the hiker on Queest Mountain. Staffing gap or funding gap, either way the actual protective layer is a specific person answering emails and posting warning signs at trailheads, and when that person is gone, the region defaults back to exactly what didn't work yesterday.

00:13:07.409 --> 00:13:34.993
<v Riley>Zoom out further, then, because The Tyee's piece on B.C.'s extinction problem makes the same point at the provincial level. B.C. had a standalone Species at Risk Act on the table for years. It's stalled — The Narwhal reports the province has stepped back from that specific legislative path. Former minister Nathan Cullen is calling it "cautious optimism" about alternative measures, which is bureaucratic language for "we're not doing the thing we said we'd do."

00:13:35.588 --> 00:13:55.780
<v Casey>The northern spotted owl has held threatened status under the U.S. Endangered Species Act since June 1990. Thirty-six years. It's still declining. That's the patchwork approach playing out as a single, decades-long case study — a bit of Wildlife Act here, a bit of Forest and Range Practices Act there, and nobody actually accountable for the whole picture.

00:13:56.399 --> 00:14:21.551
<v Riley>A patchwork can move faster than one omnibus law in specific cases, though. The conservation hatchery west of Williams Lake we covered earlier this week — that's provincial staff, First Nations, and community stewardship groups all working the same salmon population without waiting on a unified species act to exist first. That's real, active protection happening under exactly the fragmented system you're calling broken.

00:14:22.098 --> 00:14:46.290
<v Casey>That hatchery has a named site, a defined population, and visible funding attached to it. The spotted owl has none of those things — it's diffuse, it spans jurisdictions, and it's been sitting in the same regulatory limbo for over three decades. Patchwork governance works fine when someone's already paying close attention to that specific piece. It fails everywhere the attention isn't pointed, because nobody's assigned to own the failure.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:15:10.908
<v Riley>Which might be the actual distinction worth drawing out of today's material. It's not gear versus law, and it's not self-sufficiency versus connection as some abstract debate. It's whether a given species, or a given stretch of forest service road, has a defined owner — a name, a mandate — or whether responsibility is spread thin enough that everyone quietly assumes somebody else has it covered.

00:15:11.571 --> 00:15:39.779
<v Casey>Which brings the poaching story into it. Business Insider's piece on the undercover investigator, Allison Skidmore — she's not out there with better hiking boots or a sharper GPS unit. She's infiltrating trafficking networks, because wildlife crime runs on networks: buyers, launderers, corrupt officials moving product and money across borders. One self-sufficient ranger with a rifle and a radio doesn't crack that. It takes cross-agency intelligence sharing, and patience measured in years, not seasons.

00:15:40.179 --> 00:15:51.499
<v Riley>That one I'll concede outright, no counter. Poaching is organized crime wearing a wildlife theme, and organized crime has never once been solved by an individual, however well-equipped, working alone.

00:15:52.133 --> 00:16:10.133
<v Casey>So that's two out of three landing on the connected side of the ledger. Bear conflict needs an enforced rule and a coordinator watching activity. Poaching needs an intelligence network across agencies. Species protection needs a defined legal owner. Where does your self-sufficiency case actually hold up, at this point?

00:16:10.453 --> 00:16:30.677
<v Riley>In the ten seconds after something's already gone wrong, on the ground, for one person. That's real, and it matters enormously to the hiker standing there. But you're right that it doesn't scale up to population-level protection. Those are different problems sitting at different altitudes, and it's a mistake to grade one by the other's standard.

00:16:31.252 --> 00:16:46.484
<v Casey>Right. Individual preparedness and species-level protection aren't the same fight. Treating them as one thing is exactly how the province gets to sound like it's doing something on wildlife while the actual structural piece — a defined owner, an enforced rule — never gets built.

00:16:47.384 --> 00:17:03.240
<v Riley>So what does an answer specific to the Cariboo actually look like here. Not Banff's budget. Not Nelson's now-vacant coordinator position. Something the Cariboo Regional District could plausibly do without waiting on a provincial statute that's already stalled once.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:32.880
<v Casey>The CRD already has authority to set rules on forest service roads in partnership with the province. There's a concrete ask sitting right there — a leash requirement on high-traffic roads during confirmed grizzly activity, posted physically at the trailhead, tied to a sightings log that's actually kept current. Scout Island's field naturalists already run bird counts on a volunteer basis. That same model could feed a live bear-activity map that people check before they head out, instead of finding out the hard way on the road itself.

00:17:33.428 --> 00:17:46.764
<v Riley>That's a fix that doesn't need a grant cycle or a legislative session. It needs the CRD to decide this falls inside their job, and it needs one group willing to keep the map current after the news coverage fades.

00:17:47.322 --> 00:17:58.578
<v Casey>Whether anybody actually keeps that map updated once this stops being a story is the open question. It's always the open question with this kind of infrastructure — easy to launch, easy to let go quiet.

00:17:59.102 --> 00:18:15.678
<v Riley>If anyone in the Shuswap or here in the Cariboo knows how that Queest Mountain road is actually signed, or whether a leash bylaw for forest service roads has ever been proposed for our region, send it in. Corrections, tips, anything — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:18:16.013 --> 00:18:19.941
<v Casey>And if you're heading out this long weekend, leash the dog. Have a good one.

00:18:20.605 --> 00:18:25.749
<v Riley>Enjoy your weekend, and check the trailhead sign on your way past it, not just on your way back.